---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PageHeader from "../../components/PageHeader.astro";
---

<BaseLayout title="Neutrality Policy" description="How we stay politically, religiously, and culturally neutral, and how we decide what's worth including.">
  <PageHeader eyebrow="Site Info" title="Neutrality Policy" description="Last updated: September 2026" />
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12 sm:px-6">
    <div class="prose max-w-none prose-headings:font-display">
      <p>
        Bent Grass Neighborhood is a resident-run, volunteer-managed communication project. We're not affiliated
        with any political party, campaign, religious organization, HOA, or metropolitan district. This page
        explains how we decide what to include in our newsletter, website, and other communications — so residents
        and contributors alike know what to expect from us.
      </p>

      <h2>Our Position</h2>
      <p>
        We aim to be a politically, religiously, and culturally neutral resource for the neighborhood. We don't
        endorse candidates, campaigns, ballot measures, religious institutions, or advocacy causes, and we don't
        take sides on contested community issues. Our only test for including something is simple: is it likely to
        be useful or of interest to residents?
      </p>

      <h2>How This Applies</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Elections &amp; government:</strong> We share nonpartisan, factual information — voter registration
        deadlines, polling locations, official meeting notices and agendas. We don't publish content that advocates
        for a candidate, party, or ballot position.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Religious &amp; cultural organizations:</strong> We're happy to list events hosted by religious or
        cultural groups — a food drive, a resource fair, a community meal — when they're open to and benefit the
        broader neighborhood. Listing an event isn't an endorsement of the host's beliefs, and choosing not to list
        one isn't a statement against them.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Local businesses &amp; sponsors:</strong> Business mentions and sponsorships are neighbor services,
        not endorsements of a business's practices, ownership, or affiliations.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Development &amp; growth topics:</strong> For proposed developments, schools, or other projects
        moving through county or district review, we share factual project information and links to the official
        review process, so residents can stay informed and form their own opinions.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Editorial discretion:</strong> We may decline or edit submissions that are inflammatory,
        discriminatory, deceptive, or written mainly to persuade rather than inform.
      </p>

      <h2>What This Means in Practice</h2>
      <p>
        Because this policy applies neighborhood-wide, individual newsletter items, event listings, and sponsor
        mentions don't carry their own separate disclaimers. If you're curious how a specific listing fits our
        approach, this page is the explanation — we link back to it rather than repeating it every time.
      </p>

      <h2>How We Decide on Event Listings</h2>
      <p>
        We judge every submission on its content and purpose — never on who's submitting it. A request from any
        group, regardless of religion, political affiliation, or background, is held to the same standard.
      </p>
      <p>
        Generally, we're glad to include an event if it's genuinely open to the neighborhood, described in a
        factual and welcoming way, and something residents would want to know about.
      </p>
      <p>We'll typically ask for changes or decline a listing if it:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Promotes or campaigns for a candidate, party, or ballot measure</li>
        <li>
          Excludes the public in a way tied to someone's background rather than serving a real, specific group
          (support groups and interest-based gatherings are always welcome)
        </li>
        <li>Is really a sales pitch or recruitment effort dressed up as a community event</li>
        <li>Involves something unsafe, illegal, or against local fire/HOA rules</li>
        <li>Includes unverified claims, alarmist language, or targets a specific person or business</li>
        <li>Isn't something Bent Grass residents could reasonably attend or benefit from</li>
      </ul>
      <p>If you're ever unsure whether your event fits, reach out — we're happy to talk it through before you submit.</p>

      <h2>Questions?</h2>
      <p>
        Contact us at <a href="mailto:bentgrassneighborhood@gmail.com">bentgrassneighborhood@gmail.com</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
